  - Ensemble Learning
  - Part-Of-Speech Features
  - WELFake
  - XGBoost
  - TextCNN
  - CPU Inference
  - Computational Eﬀiciency
abstract: >-
  Most recent work on fake news detection reaches for transformer models, which perform well but need a GPU
  and a good deal of time to train. This paper asks a simpler question: how much accuracy is lost when those
  resources are not available? We combine an XGBoost model over TF-IDF and part-of-speech features with a
  TextCNN over frozen GloVe embeddings, and merge the two by soft voting. On the WELFake dataset of 72,134
  articles the ensemble reaches an accuracy of 97.81% ± 0.20 across five random seeds, within 1.1 percentage
  points of a fine-tuned BERT reported on the same data, using 429× fewer learned parameters. The full pipeline
  trains in 14.9 minutes and classifies an article in 256 ms on a four-core consumer CPU with no GPU. We also
  report two controls the literature on this dataset has left out. Removing publisher and wire-service tokens costs
  at most 0.82 percentage points, so the result is not publisher memorisation. The part-of-speech features carry
  a measurable syntactic signal but add no accuracy the lexical representation does not already supply, and we
  report that null result plainly.
